thread:
maya: quick q — ran into weirdness with our helm chart when values.env.secrets is set to an empty array vs an empty map. PR: https://git.redwood/example/repo/pull/1234

maya: values snippet i used:
```yaml
env:
  secrets: [] # works differently when {}
```

alex: helm/Go templating treats slices and maps differently. if you `range` over an empty slice it just skips, but some helpers do `toYaml` and an empty map vs empty list renders differently in templates. can you paste the template block?

maya: sure — simplified template:
```tpl
{{- if .Values.env.secrets }}
envFrom:
  - secretRef:
      name: {{ .Values.env.secrets.name }}
{{- end }}
```

jordan: ah — that `if .Values.env.secrets` will be truthy for non-empty map, but for an empty slice it might still be nil/false depending on how it's passed. also if you expect a list of secrets vs a single object, the access `.name` will panic for a slice.

sasha: suggestion: normalize shape or guard by kind. i usually do:
```tpl
{{- if kindIs \"slice\" .Values.env.secrets }}
{{- range .Values.env.secrets }}
- secretRef:
    name: {{ .name }}
{{- end }}
{{- else if kindIs \"map\" .Values.env.secrets }}
- secretRef:
    name: {{ .Values.env.secrets.name }}
{{- end }}
```
this avoids runtime errors and supports both shapes.

alex: +1. tiny nit: wrap names with `quote` if they can be templated (eg `{{ quote .name }}`) so you don't accidentally produce invalid YAML when someone sets a value with dashes/spaces. also for linting run `helm template` with the values file to verify: `helm template ./chart -f values.yaml --debug`

